import Doc from '@/layouts/Doc.astro'
import Command from '@/components/Command.astro'
import PipelineDiagram from '@/components/PipelineDiagram.astro'
---

<Doc
  title="Coming from Mastra"
  description="Move from Mastra's observability and workflows to mcpd: what each Mastra concept becomes, how to send your traces over OpenTelemetry, and how to run both side by side while you move."
  lede="Your traces, workflows and evaluations move to one daemon that watches any agent, in any language. This page maps each Mastra concept to its mcpd equivalent and walks through the move."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>What changes when you move</h2>
  <p>
    In Mastra, observability lives inside the framework: the application
    exports its own traces, in TypeScript, from inside the Mastra process. In
    mcpd, observability is a daemon beside your code. Your agent emits
    OpenTelemetry, and mcpd receives it, stores it and shows it to you.
  </p>
  <p>
    That one change opens the rest. mcpd records any agent, in any framework,
    in any language, against any model provider. It records the services around
    your agent as readily as the agent, with no mcpd code in your application.
    It also emits OTLP, so it fits into a pipeline you already run. See
    <a href="/docs/forwarding" class="link">Forwarding</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Durable workflows move the same way. The daemon holds the definition, the
    cursor, the journal, the retry budget, the timers and every wait. Your
    process holds the step bodies and serves them through an HTTP protocol that
    six SDKs speak: TypeScript, Python, Rust, Go, Kotlin and Elixir. The daemon
    never sees a model, a prompt or a credential, and a run outlives the process
    that started it. See <a href="/docs/workflows" class="link">Workflows</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can run mcpd yourself as one binary, or use the free hosted version at
    <a href="https://mcpd.sh/app" class="link">mcpd.sh/app</a>, where every
    feature is included.
  </p>

  <h2>Concept by concept</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>In Mastra</th><th>In mcpd</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Workflow and steps</td>
        <td>A workflow definition the daemon holds as data, and step bodies served by a <a href="/docs/workers" class="link">worker</a> in any of the six languages. A body can change without the definition changing.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Suspend and resume</td>
        <td><code>wait_event</code> holds the run until an event with its name and key arrives, or until its deadline. <code>sleep</code> and <code>sleep_until</code> hold it for a time. The daemon holds all three, and no process is alive during them.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Branches</td>
        <td>A guard on a node, which decides whether that node runs at all.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Parallel steps, foreach, loops and nested workflows</td>
        <td><code>parallel</code>, <code>foreach</code>, a bounded <code>loop</code> and <code>subworkflow</code>, each with retries, effects and limits per step.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>An agent call inside a step</td>
        <td>An <code>agent</code> step: your own loop, however many model calls, as one durable step that the trace shows as an agent turn.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>AI tracing spans</td>
        <td>Spans over OTLP from any OpenTelemetry SDK, or through the six mcpd SDKs.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Exporters</td>
        <td>Forwarding: mcpd sends the spans it stores on to any OTLP endpoint you name.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>SensitiveDataFilter</code></td>
        <td>Redaction on all six signals, on by default, before anything is stored or forwarded.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Ratio sampling</td>
        <td>Deterministic sampling by trace id, so every instance keeps the same traces.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Scorers and evals</td>
        <td>Thirteen deterministic scorers, model-graded scoring against any OpenAI-shaped endpoint, datasets captured from production runs, and experiments that compare two versions.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Storage</td>
        <td>SQLite for a laptop, Postgres or ClickHouse for a team. All three tested against a real server in CI.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Search</td>
        <td>Full text over every stored prompt and completion.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Metrics</td>
        <td>Aggregated at ingest, with exact quantiles and exemplars back to a run.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Cost</td>
        <td>Priced at ingest and stored. Cached tokens charged at their own rate. An unknown model is left unpriced rather than reported as free.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>SDKs</td>
        <td>TypeScript, Python, Rust, Go, Kotlin and Elixir, generated from one specification so they cannot drift. Each carries a workflow builder and a worker.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Workflows</td>
        <td>Nine constructs, held by the daemon: steps, agent turns, sleeps, waits on events, parallel branches, foreach, bounded loops and child runs. Retries, effects and limits are per step. A conformance suite serves the same run from all six languages.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Evaluation</td>
        <td>Twenty-two deterministic scorers, nine model-graded rubrics that score itemised verdicts, and criteria of your own against any OpenAI-shaped endpoint. Datasets captured from production runs, and experiment comparison with confidence intervals.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Licence</td>
        <td>Apache-2.0. Self-hosted with no account, or free hosted at mcpd.sh.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Redaction covers all six signals</h3>
  <p>
    mcpd redacts spans, logs, metric labels, scores, feedback and aspect notes,
    before anything is written and before anything is forwarded. A key passed
    to a structured log, or a secret quoted in a score's reason, is removed with
    the same rules as a span attribute.
  </p>
  <p>
    Key matching is a substring, so <code>awsSecretAccessKey</code> and
    <code>X-API-Key</code> are caught by the rules for <code>secret</code> and
    <code>x-api-key</code>. The built-in <code>no_leaked_secret</code> scorer
    adds a second layer: it reads a run's answer, matches on the shape of a
    credential, and reports any key that reached a completion. See
    <a href="/docs/privacy" class="link">Redaction and retention</a>.
  </p>

  <PipelineDiagram class="not-prose my-8" />

  <h3>Sampling agrees across every instance</h3>
  <p>
    mcpd hashes the trace id to decide what to keep. Every replica behind a
    load balancer reaches the same answer with no coordination, so a run that
    crosses two services is kept whole or not at all. See
    <a href="/docs/sampling" class="link">Sampling</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>One binary for everything else</h3>
  <p>
    The store and the interface are compiled into one static binary. You also
    get full-text search over every stored prompt and completion, metrics
    aggregated at ingest with exact quantiles and exemplars back to a run, and
    cost priced at ingest, with cached tokens charged at their own rate. mcpd is
    Apache-2.0.
  </p>

  <h2>How to move</h2>

  <h3>1. Send your traces to mcpd</h3>
  <p>
    If your agent already emits OpenTelemetry, this is two environment
    variables. mcpd speaks OTLP over HTTP, on the endpoints every
    OpenTelemetry SDK already knows. Traces can be JSON or protobuf; logs and
    metrics are JSON.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="point an existing OpenTelemetry setup at mcpd"
    command={`export OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4317
export OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL=http/json`}
  />

  <p>
    Set the protocol line as well as the endpoint. mcpd speaks HTTP on port
    4317, and <code>http/json</code> tells the SDK to do the same.
    <a href="/docs/opentelemetry" class="link">OpenTelemetry</a> covers this and
    the rest of the settings.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Run both side by side</h3>
  <p>
    mcpd forwards the spans it stores to the destination you already use, so
    you can compare the two on the same traces before you switch. Logs,
    metrics, scores, feedback and aspect notes stay in mcpd.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="keep the old destination while you compare"
    command="mcpd serve --forward-endpoint https://your-existing-collector"
  />

  <h3>3. Move your workflows</h3>
  <p>
    Write each workflow as a definition with the builder in your SDK, and
    register its step bodies in a worker. Where a Mastra step suspended, put a
    <code>wait_event</code>, and send the event to <code>/v1/api/events</code>
    with the name and key the wait expects. See
    <a href="/docs/runs" class="link">Runs</a> for events and waits.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Move your evaluations</h3>
  <p>
    Run the built-in scorers over stored runs, capture the production runs you care about
    into datasets, and compare versions with experiments. See
    <a href="/docs/testing" class="link">Regression testing</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    If your agent does not emit OpenTelemetry yet, start with
    <a href="/docs/quickstart" class="link">Quickstart</a>, or pick your
    language under Instrument in the sidebar.
  </p>

